import re

s = open("MANUSCRIPT_BJC.md", encoding="utf-8").read()

# ---- Intro: compress para 1+2+3 ----
old1 = """Glioblastoma (GBM) remains the most aggressive primary brain tumor in adults, with a median overall survival of approximately 15 months despite maximal resection, radiochemotherapy, and temozolomide [1,2]. Intratumoral heterogeneity spans genomic, transcriptomic, epigenetic, and metabolic layers [3,4], and therapeutic resistance emerges from this heterogeneity. Spliceosomal dysregulation is a recognized hallmark of cancer [33], and the SF3B1 modulator H3B-8800 has reached phase-I testing in myeloid neoplasms [34], and pladienolide-class compounds such as E7107 have been evaluated preclinically, while preclinical studies suggest that selected SF3B1-mutant tumors may exhibit increased sensitivity to spliceosome inhibition [35]; their role in GBM remains incompletely characterized [35,36]. Notably, reports on splicing machinery and prognosis in glioma are contradictory: transcript-level measurements associate spliceosome expression weakly or even favorably with outcome, whereas proteomic studies imply the opposite. We reasoned that this contradiction might arise from a layer-specific phenomenon — post-transcriptional accumulation of splicing machinery that transcript-level measurements may not fully capture.

What upstream program could drive such layer-specific accumulation? Histone acylation — including acetylation and lactylation of histone tails — is written by histone acetyltransferases (EP300, KAT2A, KAT2B, KAT5, KAT8, CREBBP) and erased by class I HDACs and sirtuins (HDAC1–3, SIRT1–3, SIRT6–7) [5,10–12], and the net modification state reflects the balance between these opposing enzymatic capacities. Histone acylation is disease-relevant in glioma [6–9,13–15], and several lactylation-associated prognostic signatures have been derived [27–32], but these remain at the transcriptomic correlation level. Importantly, the same enzymes also catalyze non-histone protein acetylation, including of splicing factors — a regulatory layer distinct from chromatin-level histone modification. We therefore hypothesized that writer–eraser imbalance could stabilize the spliceosome at the protein level without altering its transcription — a mechanism that may not be fully captured by transcriptomic measurements.

We therefore asked three questions: (i) Does a discrete writer–eraser imbalance state exist within GBM tumors? (ii) What is its proteomic output, is the accumulated machinery functionally active, and can it explain the transcript–protein contradiction? (iii) Is it therapeutically actionable, and where do its predictions stop? We introduce the Writer–Eraser Balance Score (WEBS), a rank-based transcriptomic score computable on single-cell and bulk data. WEBS is designed as an expression-based proxy of the relative abundance of selected acylation-associated writers and erasers, not as a direct measure of enzymatic activity or of site-specific lysine acylation. Evidence for a malignant-cell-intrinsic state derives primarily from single-cell transcriptomics, whereas spliceosome accumulation is demonstrated at the bulk-proteome level; these layers are reported separately throughout. We subject the score to multi-layer validation: single-cell and spatial transcriptomics, snATAC-seq, CPTAC TMT proteomics, acetylomics and phosphoproteomics, transcriptome-wide alternative splicing analysis, bulk clinical cohorts including the longitudinal GLASS consortium, in-silico network perturbation, CRISPR dependency screens, pharmacogenomic screens, molecular docking, pan-cancer analysis across 33 TCGA cohorts, and method-level benchmarking. Negative and boundary results are reported explicitly. An overview of the study design is shown in Fig. 1."""

new1 = """Glioblastoma (GBM) remains the most aggressive primary brain tumor in adults [1,2]. Spliceosomal dysregulation is a recognized hallmark of cancer [33], and spliceosome modulators are under preclinical and early clinical evaluation [34,35]. Notably, reports on splicing machinery and prognosis in glioma are contradictory: transcript-level measurements associate spliceosome expression weakly or even favorably with outcome, whereas proteomic studies imply the opposite. We reasoned that this contradiction might arise from post-transcriptional accumulation of splicing machinery that transcript-level measurements may not fully capture.

What upstream program could drive such accumulation? Histone acylation is written by acetyltransferases (EP300, KAT2A, KAT2B, KAT5, KAT8, CREBBP) and erased by class I HDACs and sirtuins (HDAC1–3, SIRT1–3, SIRT6–7) [5,10–12], and these enzymes also acetylate non-histone proteins, including splicing factors. We therefore hypothesized that writer–eraser imbalance could stabilize the spliceosome at the protein level without altering its transcription.

We therefore asked three questions: (i) Does a discrete writer–eraser imbalance state exist within GBM tumors? (ii) What is its proteomic output, and is the accumulated machinery functionally active? (iii) Is it therapeutically actionable? We introduce the Writer–Eraser Balance Score (WEBS), a rank-based expression proxy of writer–eraser abundance — not a direct measure of enzymatic activity or site-specific acylation. Evidence for a malignant-cell-intrinsic state derives primarily from single-cell transcriptomics, whereas spliceosome accumulation is demonstrated at the bulk-proteome level. We subject the score to multi-layer validation (single-cell, spatial, snATAC-seq, CPTAC proteomics/PTM-omics, alternative splicing, clinical cohorts including GLASS, network sensitivity analysis, pharmacogenomics, docking, pan-cancer analysis, and benchmarking), with negative and boundary results reported explicitly (Fig. 1)."""

assert old1 in s, "intro not found"
s = s.replace(old1, new1)

# ---- Methods 2.6 proteomics: re-compress (the earlier compression may have been overwritten) ----
# find current 2.6 paragraph and compress generically
m = re.search(r"(## 2\.6 Proteomics, acetylomics, and phosphoproteomics\n\n)(.*?)(\n\n## 2\.7)", s, re.S)
if m:
    new26 = """\\1limma differential abundance (LHS vs LLS) on the CPTAC proteome; spliceosome enrichment by Fisher's test (111 proteins). Layer discordance (protein vs mRNA pathway scores vs respective WEBS, Spearman) with patient-paired bootstrap CIs (2,000 resamples), leave-one-protein-out, partial correlations controlling proliferation/immune/MES/hypoxia/OPC programs, and 10,000 expression-matched random gene sets. Acetylome (13,556→8,350 sites, ≥50% quantified) and phosphoproteome (31,010 sites): per-site limma; spliceosome enrichment; per-protein coupling; SRSF/kinase analyses. PTM analyses repeated on abundance-corrected values (site − parent protein). Per-protein residuals (protein ~ mRNA per patient) tested the direct accumulation hypothesis; the complex-level residual score was compared with 2,000 expression-matched random sets.\\3"""
    s = s[:m.start()] + new26 + s[m.end():]

# ---- Results 3.4: compress the new residual sentence if too long ----
s = s.replace(
"To test directly whether spliceosome proteins exceed what their mRNAs predict, we computed per-protein mRNA-adjusted residuals (protein ~ mRNA per patient) in the paired CPTAC data: 103 of 109 spliceosome proteins showed a positive WEBS effect after mRNA adjustment (FDR < 0.05; Fisher enrichment OR = 55.9 vs genome, p = 2.9 × 10⁻⁵⁶), the complex-level residual score tracked WEBS (ρ = 0.78) and increased across groups (KW p = 1.9 × 10⁻¹³), and 105/109 proteins had higher residuals in LHS than LLS — with the residual signature exceeding 2,000 expression-matched random gene sets (empirical p = 5 × 10⁻⁴; Fig. S36).",
"To test directly whether spliceosome proteins exceed what their mRNAs predict, we computed per-protein mRNA-adjusted residuals in the paired CPTAC data: 103/109 spliceosome proteins showed positive WEBS effects after mRNA adjustment (FDR < 0.05; Fisher OR = 55.9, p = 2.9 × 10⁻⁵⁶), the complex residual score tracked WEBS (ρ = 0.78; KW p = 1.9 × 10⁻¹³), and the signature exceeded 2,000 expression-matched random sets (empirical p = 5 × 10⁻⁴; Fig. S36).")

open("MANUSCRIPT_BJC.md", "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s)

parts = re.split(r"(?m)^(# .+)$", s)
cur = ""; counts = {}
for p in parts:
    if p.startswith("# "):
        cur = p.strip(); counts.setdefault(cur, 0)
    else:
        counts[cur] = counts.get(cur, 0) + len(re.findall(r"[\w’'-]+", p))
body = sum(counts.get(k, 0) for k in ["# 1. Introduction", "# 2. Methods", "# 3. Results", "# 4. Discussion", "# 5. Conclusion"])
print("body:", body, {k: counts.get(k) for k in ["# 1. Introduction", "# 2. Methods", "# 3. Results", "# 4. Discussion", "# 5. Conclusion"]})
